    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where the ith line mean $ (\\bar{z}_i) $ has coefficient $ \\theta_{Si} $, and $ e_i $ denotes the deviation of the observed mean from the prediction of the model. In matrix form, letting $ \\bar{z} $ be the vector of observed line means, a be the $ (2 \\times 1) $ vector of effects $ \\mu_0 $ and $ \\alpha_1^c $, and M be the matrix of coefficients, the linear model becomes",
      "source_page": 232,
