        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "3eb4ef5598eb7f8c37b023c25f69c865d0cdc31d92bbe6f8f5de2658ea4d1fb0"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "If the test statistic is large enough to reject the additive model, the logical next step is to evaluate the additive-dominance model. In this case, the vector a contains a third element, $ \\delta_{1}^{c} $, and the matrix M contains a third row consisting of the elements $ \\theta_{Hi} $. The solution again follows from Equation 9.8a, and the new fit is evaluated by use of Equation 9.10, where the $ \\chi^{2} $ statistic is now distributed with k - 3 degrees of freedom, as three parameters are fitted.",
      "source_page": 233,
      "printed_page": 213,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter9:p013:b3"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        60.0,
        245.0,
        1023.0,
        469.0
      ],
      "source_pages": [
        233
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          60.0,
          245.0,
          1023.0,
          469.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "36e0c7894e1fae611908d8348c56025de34b34250e377d8aed8c059643fecd34"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Letting $ \\chi_{A}^{2} $ and $ \\chi_{AD}^{2} $ denote the test statistics associated with the additive and additive-dominance models, then the difference",
      "source_page": 233,
      "printed_page": 213,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_chapter9:p013:b4"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        57.0,
